# Methodological Profile {#sec-metodos}

Methodological diversity strengthens the robustness of conclusions. The chart shows which research approaches predominate in the corpus.

::: {#fig-methods .cell execution_count=11}
``` {.python .cell-code}
method_series = sources["method"].dropna().str.lower()

method_keywords = {
    "Survey / Questionnaire": ["survey", "questionnaire"],
    "Interview": ["interview"],
    "Literature review": ["review", "literature", "meta-analysis", "bibliometric"],
    "Case study": ["case study", "case-study"],
    "Quantitative analysis": ["regression", "sem ", "structural equation", "statistical"],
    "Mixed method": ["mixed method", "mixed-method"],
    "Experiment / Choice": ["experiment", "choice experiment", "conjoint", "contingent"],
    "Modelling / Simulation": ["model", "simulation", "agent-based", "optimization"],
    "Content analysis": ["content analysis", "discourse", "thematic", "qualitative"],
    "Secondary data": ["secondary data", "panel data", "database"],
}

method_counts = {}
for label, keywords in method_keywords.items():
    mask = method_series.apply(lambda x: any(kw in x for kw in keywords))
    method_counts[label] = mask.sum()

method_df = pd.DataFrame([
    {"Method": k, "Articles": v} for k, v in method_counts.items() if v > 0
]).sort_values("Articles", ascending=True)

fig = px.bar(
    method_df,
    x="Articles", y="Method",
    orientation="h",
    color_discrete_sequence=["#5C6BC0"],
    text="Articles",
    height=400,
)
fig.update_traces(textposition="outside")
fig.update_layout(template="plotly_white", font=dict(size=12))
fig.show()

display(Markdown(
    f"*Note: an article may use more than one method, so the totals "
    f"may exceed the {len(sources)} articles in the corpus.*"
))
```

